WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:39.000
This is Hidden Brain. I'm Shankar Vedantam. The poet Robert Frost wrote about two roads diverging in a yellow wood, a moment when a traveler must choose a path, not knowing where either one leads. In the poem, the traveler is indecisive, first looking down one path and then another. The last lines of that poem have become some of the most famous lines in American poetry. Two roads diverged in a wood, and I, I took the one less traveled by, and that has made all the difference. Perhaps you've had moments when you confronted a similar fork in the road.
这里是《隐藏的大脑》（Hidden Brain）。我是尚卡尔·维丹塔姆（Shankar Vedantam）。诗人罗伯特·弗罗斯特（Robert Frost）曾写过两条在黄树林中分叉的道路，那是旅行者必须做出选择的时刻，却不知道哪条路通向何方。在诗中，旅行者犹豫不决，先是看向一条路，然后又看向另一条。那首诗的最后几行已成为美国诗歌中最著名的诗句之一：“林中两路分叉，而我，我选择了人迹更少的一条，从此一切都变得不同。”也许你也曾遇到过类似的十字路口。

2
00:00:39.000 --> 00:01:13.000
Should you stay in the city you're living in or move? Should you stay with the person you're with or leave? Should you stick with a job you have or look for a new one? Many of us peer down the different paths before us, trying to figure out which one will make us happy. But like that traveler in the woods, we find there isn't enough evidence one way or the other. We go weeks, months, or years, paralyzed by indecision.
你应该留在现在的城市还是搬走？你应该留在现在的伴侣身边还是离开？你应该坚持现在的工作还是寻找新工作？我们许多人都在审视面前不同的道路，试图弄清楚哪一条会让我们幸福。但就像树林里的那个旅行者一样，我们发现并没有足够的证据支持任何一方。我们可能会因为优柔寡断而陷入瘫痪，持续数周、数月甚至数年。

3
00:01:13.000 --> 00:01:35.000
And then something happens. Maybe it's something big. We get a serious medical diagnosis or someone we love dies. Or maybe it's something small. You get a traffic ticket or someone looks at you funny. And all of a sudden, your dilemma is over. You know which way to go. You know which path to choose.
然后事情发生了。也许是一件大事。我们得到了严重的医疗诊断，或者我们所爱的人去世了。又或者是一件小事。你收到了一张交通罚单，或者有人用奇怪的眼神看你。突然之间，你的困境结束了。你知道该走哪条路。你知道该选择哪条路径。

4
00:01:35.000 --> 00:02:14.000
Today on the show and in a companion episode on Hidden Brain Plus, we examine the unexpected experiences that give us clarity. We look at how and why they are so powerful, and we explore techniques to make the best use of these moments. Stepping into the future and leaving the past behind. This week on Hidden Brain. All of our lives are made up by a series of moments. Most of them pass by without our notice.
今天在节目中，以及在《隐藏的大脑 Plus》的配套剧集中，我们将探讨那些带给我们清晰认知的意外经历。我们将审视它们为何如此强大，并探索如何充分利用这些时刻。迈向未来，将过去抛在身后。本周的《隐藏的大脑》。我们的一生由一系列时刻组成。其中大多数时刻都在我们毫无察觉中流逝。

5
00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:43.000
We are busy, or we are on autopilot, or we are paying attention to something else. But all of us also have moments that stand out. At these times, you can suddenly see your own life clearly. You know what to do going forward. At University College London, psychologist Anthony Klott studies these moments. He spent years examining how they inform what we do and how they can reveal whom we want to become. Anthony Klotz, welcome to Hidden Brain.
我们很忙，或者处于自动驾驶模式，或者在关注其他事情。但我们每个人也都有一些脱颖而出的时刻。在这些时刻，你可以突然清晰地看到自己的人生。你知道未来该做什么。在伦敦大学学院，心理学家安东尼·克洛茨（Anthony Klotz）研究这些时刻。他花了多年时间研究它们如何影响我们的行为，以及它们如何揭示我们想要成为什么样的人。安东尼·克洛茨，欢迎来到《隐藏的大脑》。

